A||N||10||Monkeyman||A quick question about shocks||||5||
Z||000000||Monkeyman||08-06-2000||04:07 AM||||I'm planning to replace my shocks on the Grape Ape when I do the brakes.  I want to be prepared so...<P>What kind of hardware (nuts, bolts, etc) will I need to replace the shocks?  I'm just going to assume that all of the hardware will be rusty and will break.  I'd rather buy something and not have to use it than to try and scramble to get it while my car's apart.<P>While I'm on the subject of shocks...What preparations need to be made on the shocks to paint them purple?  They're white now.  Red pads might look cool with purple calipers, but I don't want a rainbow effect.  (Different colors for each component.)  Can I just clean them well and spray, or do I need to lightly sand them down 1st?<P>------------------<BR>Dave-<BR>Grape Ape ('85GT)||||reg||5
Z||000001||stimpy||08-06-2000||02:40 PM||||Good question. I'd hate to see it not be replied to.||||reg||1
Z||000002||BAM-BAM||08-06-2000||02:58 PM||||   Monk, I would imagine that like any smooth metalic surface, there would need to be some ruffing done to provide a surface that the paint can adhere to. The shocks, being a sealed unit, should exhibit no adverse  functional anomalies. Using a fine grit sand paper to just scuff the surface should be fine. You might also want to prime the surface with a good quality primer. Just my .02.<P> Late, BAM!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>------------------<BR>Don B. <BR>85GT||||reg||1
Z||000003||SCCA FIERO||08-06-2000||02:59 PM||||I tried to find the bolt/nuts in the 22P manual, but only could find the lower bolt and nut. Here is what's listed.<P>Bolt, hex (M10x1.5x60). Which means it's a 10mm with a 1.5 pitch thread and 60mm long.<P>Nut, hex slotted (M10x1) 10PC (not sure about that, but it goes on the end of that bolt.<P>I would get the old ones out and take them to the local Hardware store to find matches to them.<P>As far as the painting goes: I would give them a good roughing up with some fine grit sandpaper to give the new paint something to adhear to. I would then clean them with some good cleaner (Acetone) and then paint. Don't forget to cover the bushings up so you don't paint them... that would look stupid.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000004||theogre||08-06-2000||03:52 PM||||There's a prep product used to paint car bodies. It removes wax oil etc. I'd give the shocks a light scuff sand then clean with the prep. It goes by a number of names none of which I can remember.<P>front shocks usually come with everything you need. <P>struts are a whole nother ball of wax. At the least you'll likely need a bolt kit for the bottom of the struts if the ones there now are the originals. You May also need/want bumpers boots and maybe the top bearing unit. (Top bearings can still get crappy on fiero even tho they don't get much use.) Bumpers should be replaced if they are beatup, dry rotted, etc. Boots are optional and they come in diferant colors. Boots just keep crud off the top of the strut.||||reg||1
Z||000005||2birds||08-06-2000||10:34 PM||||Most suspension stuff uses grade 10.9 hardware, too.  Kind of like the metric version of Grade 5.  Not so easy to find at Home Depot and Lowe's.  I found them at Pep Boys for my control arm bushing bolts and my pressure plate.  The strut-to-knuckle bolts are probably a dealer part, but Moog and others sell them, sometimes offset for camber adjustment, although usually not necessary with aftermarket struts (like my Monroes), since the lower bolt hole is usually slotted instead.||||reg||1
Z||000006||Cooter||08-06-2000||11:29 PM||||To prep the shocks for painting, and if you don't have any prep cleaner- windex will do ONLY if you use it before sanding to keep the oil residue out of the sand scratches. Get a red or green "Scotch Brite Pad" and squirt a little dish washing soap on it and then wet it. Scrub the shock body with the wet/soapy scuff pad. Rinse and dry the parts and they are ready to paint.<P>------------------<BR>The slower they think you are, the more suprised they will be when you smoke them.||||reg||1
Z||000007||Monkeyman||08-07-2000||01:53 AM||||Thanx for the paint prep ideas.  I can get a Scotchbrite pad (green) from work and I have some 400- and 600-grit sandpaper left from another project.<P>The shocks (not worried about the struts right now--they'll go on once I get my new springs) didn't come with any hardware.  They have the regular eyelet at the top (I assume it's the top), but there's a short "tube" with flattened ends (drilled out) at the other end.  (Does this sound familiar to anyone???)  I guess I can buy whatever I need once I break the old hardware, but I'd rather have everything I might possibly need right at my fingertips.  Less frustration that way.  (I get frustrated easily.)  Once it stops raining, I'll have to remove a wheel and see if I can figure out what goes where.<P>You guys have been a great help.  I could probably answer my own questions if I could just find my 22P.  I haven't been able to find it since I moved my computer.  Dryers eat socks.  Maybe my computer ate my 22P.||||reg||1
Z||000008||2birds||08-07-2000||09:46 PM||||Monkeyman, you have it upside down on the front shocks.  The flattened part goes up top.  All you need is a 13 mm socket and 6" worth of extension for the top bolts, and a pair of 15 mm for the bottom bolt.  Some liquid wrench will probably save the bolts.  Once you pull the wheel off, you'll see it's a no-brainer. <P>Make sure you don't get any overspray on the piston rod.||||reg||1
Z||000009||Monkeyman||08-08-2000||01:59 AM||||Thanx, 2birds.  I just realized that I don't have any boots.  Any idea where I can get some white or purple boots???||||reg||1
Z||000010||2birds||08-09-2000||12:30 AM||||4X4's use them all the time, and I saw Monroe has them on wrenchhead's price list.  Problem is, what size shock body do they fit?  The fronts don't normally have them, but if you can find ones that fit...<BR>The struts do have a boot, and they're probably trashed anyway.  Same source could probably get them, too.  ||||reg||1
